winebus: share a DualSense with macOS unless its key asks for the seize.

mgvf-0006 opened a DualSense on Bluetooth with kIOHIDOptionsTypeSeizeDevice by
default, written against the link drops seen under a shared open on
2026-09-08. That the seize prevented them was never measured, and its own
costs were: macOS's 900 s idle cut lands in the middle of a game, because a
seized pad shows macOS's plugin no input, and macOS and its applications lose
the pad for as long as the bottle is up.

WHAT WAS MEASURED with SeizeDevice 0, a DualSense Edge (054c:0df2) over
Bluetooth, on 2026-09-14 and 2026-09-15:

- three sessions of play, of 8, 25 and 34 minutes: no "Unable to send BT
  output report", no "device is exclusive access" and no idle cut during play.
  The plugin's idle clock reset with the input the trace showed;
- a 24-hour Bluetooth log, from 11:18 to 11:18: seven disconnects, each with a
  cause. Four followed a feature 0x08 power-off request (ACL 10719); three
  were macOS's idle cut (10722), each well after play had ended, so a session
  left behind does not keep a shared pad awake;
- the 34-minute session, by the user: the PS button, the lights and the rumble
  work. Its one disconnect came from the pad's side (HID reason 431), and the
  pad came back with the game still running;
- macOS's plugin attached at both of that session's connects, the reconnect
  included, while wine already held the pad shared.

THE CHANGE, in bus_iohid.c. want_seize's default is FALSE for every device.
The value is unchanged in meaning: 0 opens shared, non-zero seizes, and it
still decides it for any device its key names. A shared open is now traced
("Opened HID device ... shared with macOS"), the counterpart of the seize's
own line, so a trace says which open a pad got; RaccoonBot also looks for
that string to tell an engine that shares by default.

WHAT FOLLOWS FROM IT:

- macOS repaints the lightbar of a shared pad whenever the front application
  changes, which undoes mgvf-0031's one rewritten colour. mgvf-0035 answers
  that;
- mgvf-0033's power-off acts only on a seized pad, so by default it does
  nothing; macOS's own idle cut turns off a shared pad nobody is using;
- a pad on a cable was never seized, and nothing changes for it.

NOT CLAIMED: that sharing causes no drop anywhere -- one pad, one Mac, three
sessions and one day; that the frame cost of a shared open is the same, which
was not measured; anything about the 2026-09-08 timeouts, whose log is gone.

